+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a human-friendly length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is
+either a whole number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups, such as `2d`, `1h30m`, or
+`1d2h3m4s`. The units are `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds). Each unit may be used at most once,
+and units must be given in that order. Units are case-insensitive, so `45S` is the same as `45s`. Spaces, decimals, and
+negative values are rejected. A {class}`~datetime.timedelta` passed as a default or from Python is used as is.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=30m1h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
